    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Requiem For A Village is Steven Wilson’s ninth solo studio album, a progressive and art-rock meditation on an isolated, fictional British community. Its atmosphere is pastoral yet unsettling, combining Gothic folk, doom-laden guitar, analogue textures, and carefully shaped progressive-rock dynamics. The album’s sense of place is reinforced by themes of lost traditions, memory, folklore, and “hiraeth”—a profound longing for a home or past that may never have existed.\n\nThe Village Green vinyl edition presents the album as a distinctive two-LP release on Fiction Records, preserving Wilson’s organic, deliberately human-sounding production across an expansive sequence that includes the ominous title track, the patient “On Chapel Slope,” the dramatic “A Basket Of Bees,” and the closing suite “The Old God Is Buried.” Critics have highlighted its eerie, storybook quality and its evocative blend of pastoral beauty with sinister, Sabbath-like weight, making it a particularly atmospheric entry in Wilson’s progressive-rock catalogue.",
